00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.510
Political alliances manifest themselves here—on
the voting floor of the United Nations General

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.900
Assembly in New York, New York.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.960
In this room countries impose legislation
that carries weight worldwide and so what

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.400
happens in this room is the physical materialization
of the world’s politics.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:30.170
47 years ago, exactly that happened when one
of the General Assembly’s most consequential

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:31.350
votes occurred.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.450
China, you see, essentially has two governments.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:39.060
There’s the Republic of China which used
to control the mainland and Taiwan but today

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.800
only administers Taiwan and there’s the
People's Republic of China which controls

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:43.960
the mainland.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.050
Both claim to be the rightful governments
of all of the Chinese territory—both Taiwan

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:52.850
and the mainland—and so back in 1971 the
United Nations had to decide which government

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.010
would represent China.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:58.510
Essent ially, the question was which government
was the rightful leader of the territory as

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.300
there could only be one in the United Nations.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.610
The US was the main superpower opposing the
People’s Republic representing China as

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:09.900
it had a strong political and military alliance
with the Republic of China government and

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.420
so the vote was essentially the US’ sphere
of influence versus the world.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:19.020
Among the 35 countries that voted against
the People’s Republic were much of Africa—the

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.350
Central African Republic, Chad, Gabon, Liberia,
Niger, South Africa, and plenty of others

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.170
that sided with the US.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:29.840
Despite the US’s efforts, the resolution
ended up passing and the representative government

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.610
for China in the UN was switched to the People’s
Republic of China but what’s interesting

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:38.640
about this is not the result, it’s who voted
against the People’s Republic.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.640
Since that 1971 vote, you see, something has
changed.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.960
In 2007, the UN general assembly met once
again to vote on whether to adopt a resolution

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.950
condemning the human rights situation in North
Korea.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.760
As one of North Korea’s strongest allies,
this vote was China and its sphere of influence

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.530
versus the world.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:02.760
In this vote, though, only Burundi, Equatorial
Guinea, Eritrea, Ghana, Kenya, Lesotho, Liberia,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.920
Madagascar, Malawi, Mauritania, and Tanzania
voted against China.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:12.200
All the 43 other African countries either
abstained or voted no along with China because

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.780
in the forty years between those votes, political
ties changed.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.450
Africa no longer bows to the US.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:23.340
Much of the continent is now economically
and politically aligned with the world’s

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.209
fastest emerging superpower—China.

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:30.260
The simple answer for why this is is because
China has pumped huge amounts of money into

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.700
the continent of Africa.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.000
They’re buying allies.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:39.310
For example, China built a $3.2 billion railway
in Kenya trekking the 300 miles from Nairobi,

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:44.709
the capitol, to Mombassa, the second largest
city and primary port, in 4 hours and 30 minutes.

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:48.800
That’s faster than what the fastest train
in the US, the Acela Express, takes to travel

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.910
the equivalent distance from Philadelphia
to Boston.

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:56.940
China also built a $526 million dam in Guinea
which helped push the country from having

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:01.690
constant power shortages to making more energy
than it needs and selling the extra capacity

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:03.120
to its neighbors.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.849
China also built a $475 million light rail
system in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, the first

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:12.590
of its kind in sub-Saharan Africa, designed
as a way to combat the capital’s crippling

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:13.660
traffic.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.390
These are only a sampling.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:19.709
There are literally hundreds of others of
Chinese infrastructure projects in Africa

00:03:19.709 --> 00:03:20.760
each year.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.940
All across the continent, China is playing
a part in projects both big and small that

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:27.099
are transforming African economies.

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:31.180
It’s important to note that these projects
are not, though, free.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:34.989
Each of these three were financed by loans
granted by China’s state-owned and controlled

00:03:34.989 --> 00:03:39.170
Export-Import bank and these loans do, of
course, need to be paid back by the countries

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:40.380
granted them.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:44.220
Large African infrastructure projects, though,
would be viewed as risky by any traditional

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:48.910
bank and would therefore struggle to get financed
but China’s export-import bank doesn’t

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:49.910
care.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:53.400
Assuming cooperation between the Chinese and
African countries’ governments, this bank

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.640
will give low-interest or no-interest loans
to African countries so they can build these

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:00.379
trains or dams or other projects.

00:04:00.379 --> 00:04:04.030
These loans are therefore considered a form
of foreign aid since China doesn’t expect

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.750
to get all their money back, at least adjusted
for inflation, since they’re not charging

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.599
much interest and there’s a high risk of
default.

00:04:10.599 --> 00:04:14.260
Of course China isn’t just financing these
projects out of kindness.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.959
For each of them there’s a political goal
behind it.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:20.669
You see, the country of China is running out
of growth potential.

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:25.159
Its era of double digit year over year GDP
growth is over as it makes the shift from

00:04:25.159 --> 00:04:27.639
industrializing to industrialized.

00:04:27.639 --> 00:04:32.779
Africa, meanwhile, is one of the least developed
areas of the world and a lack of development

00:04:32.779 --> 00:04:35.360
actually makes fast growth easy.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:40.080
The first step of economic development for
many countries is natural resource exploitation.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.589
Nearly every country has some level of natural
resources that they can use to kickstart growth

00:04:44.589 --> 00:04:48.199
but first they need to have enough money to
build the infrastructure and take the steps

00:04:48.199 --> 00:04:50.770
needed to gather these natural resources.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:55.240
As everyone knows, it takes money to make
money and China has money.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.949
By investing in African mining and farming,
China can profit off of Africa’s growth

00:04:58.949 --> 00:05:03.259
and fuel the business back in China that require
minerals and food, but in addition to it serving

00:05:03.259 --> 00:05:07.520
as a source of natural resources, Africa has
another resource—labor.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.419
It might seem strange that China, the country
that the world uses for low-cost manufacturing,

00:05:12.419 --> 00:05:16.860
is looking for a labor source elsewhere but
that is exactly what it’s doing.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.439
China is a victim of its own success.

00:05:19.439 --> 00:05:23.349
The economic development that its manufacturing
industry brought pushed a large segment of

00:05:23.349 --> 00:05:26.909
its population into the middle class which
raised labor costs country-wide.

00:05:26.909 --> 00:05:32.180
It’s not bad news, China as a country has
shifted from having a low-skilled to a medium-skilled

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:37.879
workforce as their education level has improved,
but for the lowest cost, lowest skilled manufacturing

00:05:37.879 --> 00:05:41.069
work, the country of China is no longer competitive.

00:05:41.069 --> 00:05:46.059
Therefore, Chinese manufacturing firms are
setting up their own operations in Africa—one

00:05:46.059 --> 00:05:49.430
of the cheapest and lowest skilled labor markets
in the world.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:53.610
Today, China is now the largest trading partner
with Africa as a whole.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:58.279
Despite China being a vastly larger country
than the US in population, the US and China

00:05:58.279 --> 00:06:01.999
both trade a similar value of goods worldwide
each year.

00:06:01.999 --> 00:06:06.029
In this case, though, whereas $48 billion
worth off goods were exchanged between the

00:06:06.029 --> 00:06:13.190
US and Africa in 2016, China traded $128 billion
worth of goods—nearly three times as much.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:17.379
Now, the whole idea of setting up a structure
of power over other less developed states

00:06:17.379 --> 00:06:22.060
in order to gather resources and use their
labor force might sound familiar because that’s

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:24.000
largely what colonialism was.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.939
The motives behind European powers expanding
their territory to less developed nations

00:06:27.939 --> 00:06:32.869
in the 15th through 20th centuries were remarkably
similar to the motivations behind China’s

00:06:32.869 --> 00:06:35.839
growing economic influence in the developing
world today.

00:06:35.839 --> 00:06:40.719
Despite what some may say, there is empirical
evidence that China has been using these infrastructure

00:06:40.719 --> 00:06:43.090
investments to affect worldwide politics.

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:47.710
It’s been found that if an African country
recognizes Taiwan as a country they receive,

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:52.599
on average, 2.7 fewer Chinese infrastructure
projects within their borders each year.

00:06:52.599 --> 00:06:57.649
Conversely, if an African country votes overwhelmingly
along with China in the United Nations General

00:06:57.649 --> 00:07:02.339
Assembly, they receive 1.8 more infrastructure
projects per year.

00:07:02.339 --> 00:07:05.749
Considering that the General Assembly is an
equal representative body where each country

00:07:05.749 --> 00:07:09.969
gets one vote no matter if they have a million
residents or a billion, China’s getting

00:07:09.969 --> 00:07:14.559
a lot of influence for, in the grand scheme
of things, not a lot of money.

00:07:14.559 --> 00:07:18.439
China touts the fact that their foreign investment
and aid is “no strings attached.”

00:07:18.439 --> 00:07:22.300
Unlike other institutions that give low or
no-interest loans to developing countries

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:26.460
like the International Monetary Fund or World
Bank, China give loans with no requirements

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:29.899
on factors like respect of human rights or
democratic elections.

00:07:29.899 --> 00:07:33.969
Of course, this data linking infrastructure
investment with political leanings shows that

00:07:33.969 --> 00:07:38.599
there are indeed hidden strings that require
benefit for China rather than benefit for

00:07:38.599 --> 00:07:40.399
the receiving country.

00:07:40.399 --> 00:07:43.999
Western powers are understandably concerned
about this shift in power dynamics towards

00:07:43.999 --> 00:07:47.029
a country with vastly different ideals.

00:07:47.029 --> 00:07:51.819
In 2017, China entered a select club as it
opened a military base in Djibouti.

00:07:51.819 --> 00:07:57.509
While four other countries have bases in Djibouti—France,
Italy, Japan, and the US—this base was unique

00:07:57.509 --> 00:08:02.789
as it was China’s first base abroad and
those by themselves, military bases abroad,

00:08:02.789 --> 00:08:03.919
are unique.

00:08:03.919 --> 00:08:08.250
Only 15 of the world’s most developed and
militarily powerful countries worldwide have

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.819
them and now China is one of them.

00:08:10.819 --> 00:08:14.479
Although, western powers might be worried
for the wrong reasons.

00:08:14.479 --> 00:08:18.849
The government of China is clearly putting
a lot of focus and money into Africa but not

00:08:18.849 --> 00:08:22.689
as much as you’d think based off the result
they’re getting—vast amounts of influence

00:08:22.689 --> 00:08:24.530
over a whole continent.

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:26.499
There are two key numbers to look at.

00:08:26.499 --> 00:08:31.119
In 2015, China loaned just $12 billion to
African countries.

00:08:31.119 --> 00:08:36.020
In the same year, the country invested a mere
$3 billion in the continent.

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:40.460
That’s just not much but the reason China
is gaining this enormous influence over the

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:44.960
continent is because the Chinese government
no longer has to force this phenomenon.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:48.640
Private Chinese industry is taking hold of
Africa.

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:54.380
Of the estimated 10,000 Chinese businesses
in Africa, 90% of them are privately owned

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:57.570
rather than one of the numerous Chinese state-run
companies.

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:02.120
The Chinese companies in Africa are actually
making money—some substantially so.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:05.870
The Chinese government certainly has provided
a considerable push to the industrialization

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:11.590
of Africa but now that that’s done, economic
forces are moving the initiative further forward.

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:14.460
Chinese small business is gripping the continent.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:18.070
Much of the western world is ignoring the
prospects of the continent—ignoring that

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:22.460
business in Africa can now be as profitable
as business in China was when its period of

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:24.190
tremendous growth began.

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:29.310
Right now, Africa is establishing itself as
the source of labor and resources for China

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:34.130
and so, until the west pays attention, Africa
will continue inching forward on its path

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:36.900
towards becoming China’s China.

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:40.680
